
  [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m has neither failure mode, which is exactly why it's the right clock for interval math. (One
  correction to the reviewer's framing and to the existing docstring: DST isn't a hazard here — [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m is UTC epoch
  seconds and doesn't shift on DST transitions. NTP and manual clock sets are the real risk. The docstring overstates
  the case, and I can tighten that wording.)

  The underlying need — correlating limiter events with wall-clock log timestamps — is legitimate and solvable without
  changing the window math. Two options:

  - Record a wall-clock timestamp [3malongside[0m the monotonic one, used only for logging/emission, never for the cutoff
    comparison.
  - Log [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m at the call site when a decision is made, and leave the limiter's internals monotonic.

  I'd default to the second, since it keeps the limiter free of logging concerns. Want me to wire one of these up?

  [1m3. Pluggable storage backend — pushing back on YAGNI grounds.[0m I grepped the repo for [38;5;153mredis|backend|storage[39m: the only
  hit is [38;5;153mbuild-backend = "hatchling.build"[39m in [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m. There's no Redis dependency, no second implementation, and
  no caller that needs one. [38;5;153mdependencies = [][39m today.

  Building the interface now means guessing at the seam before the constraint that shapes it exists, and a Redis sliding
  window is not a drop-in swap for a [38;5;153mdeque[39m. The current [38;5;153mallow()[39m does read-evict-count-append as a local,
  single-threaded, atomic sequence. Against Redis that has to become one atomic server-side operation — a
  [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m/[38;5;153mZADD[39m sequence in a Lua script or [38;5;153mMULTI[39m — or concurrent gateway processes will race past the
  limit. It also brings in network failure and timeout handling, which the in-memory version has no concept of. An
  interface designed against the [38;5;153mdeque[39m today will almost certainly be the wrong shape for that, and we'd rewrite it
  anyway plus carry the abstraction in the meantime.

  My recommendation is to defer until there's an actual Redis requirement, then extract the interface from two real
  implementations. If there's a concrete plan I'm not aware of — a scheduled multi-process gateway rollout, say — that
  changes the calculus and I'll build it; tell me the target and I'll design against it rather than against a
  hypothetical.

  Item 1 is committed to the working tree, unstaged. Items 2 and 3 are untouched pending your call.

